f the russian state so  freeze by freezing their money you're  more or less just freezing russian state  money i think both of those arguments  suggest that what we're trying to do is  trying to find policy leavers that allow  us to put pressure if not on putin  himself then on the people around him by  depriving them of all the toys the bling  the luxury goods the football clubs and  you know i think it's too early to say  if they'll work but  i think it's probably worth a shot it's  pretty better than most of the other  alternative options we've got  let's talk about putin himself we know  his personal wealth is very well hidden  how can sanctions effect effectively  target him if at  all putin  i'm not sure it even makes sense to talk  about putin being wealthy i think if  russia is your current account you don't  really need a deposit account i don't  think if he has offshore wealth anywhere  then you know it's probably not very  much in comparison to the other  oligarchs the other oligarchs have huge  offshore wealth you know perhaps half of  all the wealth owned by russians is  owned offshore by oligarchs if these  oligarchs you know want to get back to  those cushy lifestyles what do they need  to do what needs to happen or has the  world's relationship with russian  oligarchs really changed forever  i'd be surprised if our relationship  with russian oligarchs had changed  forever  western countries britain in particular  but not only britain have been  historically very welcome to work to  bring in anyone with money however  we need to recognize that that as long  as this crisis lasts there will be  sanctions on russia and it will be much  harder for oligarchs to come here and  spend their money so i suppose the  answer is what western politicians want  the answer to be which is if you want to  come back and enjoy your bling in our  capital cities you're going to have to  tell putin to stop killing ukrainians um  and that  is the challenge how many of these 